              "id": "d8a9460c-6f7a-3c34-b1b2-f775eb3d4ce6",
              "name": "Exclusion of Unreadable Files from Scans Due to High False Positive Rate; Recommendation for Storage in CDN or Cloud Buckets",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"Exclusion of Unreadable Files from Scans Due to High False Positive Rate; Recommendation for Storage in CDN or Cloud Buckets\" refers to a situation where certain files are excluded from security scans due to their unreadability, which can lead to a high rate of false positives. This can occur in JavaScript programming when the code is obfuscated or minified to such an extent that it becomes unreadable, making it difficult for security tools to accurately scan for vulnerabilities. This can lead to false positives, where the tools incorrectly identify secure code as being vulnerable.\n\nIn the provided code, the vulnerability may arise if the security scanning tool is unable to accurately read and interpret the minified JavaScript code, leading to potential false positives.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to:\n\n1. Avoid obfuscating or minifying code to such an extent that it becomes unreadable. While these techniques can help to reduce the size of the code and potentially protect intellectual property, they can also make it difficult for security tools to accurately scan the code.\n\n2. Use a Content Delivery Network (CDN) or cloud storage buckets to store and deliver files. These platforms often have built-in security features that can help to protect files from common web vulnerabilities.\n\n3. Regularly update and patch all software, libraries, and dependencies to ensure that they are protected against known vulnerabilities.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code is minified and obfuscated, making it difficult to provide a specific source code fix. However, a general recommendation would be to de-minify and de-obfuscate the code to make it more readable, which would allow security tools to more accurately scan for vulnerabilities.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code does not appear to have any explicit library dependencies.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [CWE-116: Improper Encoding or Escaping of Output](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/116.html)\n\nPlease note that these links are subject to change and may not be accessible to all users."
              },
