              "id": "2b54614b-cd0d-3605-bc52-a13de770de85",
              "name": "Prototype Pollution Vulnerability Detected",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nPrototype Pollution is a vulnerability that occurs when the properties of JavaScript's `Object.prototype` are modified. The properties of `Object.prototype` are accessible to all objects. Therefore, the modification of these properties can affect all objects and potentially lead to application-wide issues or security vulnerabilities.\n\nIn JavaScript, all objects inherit properties and methods from a prototype. The `Object.prototype` is on the top of the prototype inheritance chain. Some JavaScript functions allow the modification of prototype properties. If these functions are used improperly, an attacker can inject properties into existing JavaScript language construct prototypes.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate Prototype Pollution vulnerabilities, you should:\n\n- Avoid using functions that can modify an object's prototype in an unsafe way. These functions include `Object.setPrototypeOf()`, `Object.__proto__`, and `Object.prototype.__defineGetter__()`.\n\n- Validate and sanitize all user inputs. This can prevent an attacker from injecting malicious values.\n\n- Use a linter like ESLint to detect and prevent the use of functions that can lead to Prototype Pollution.\n\n- Keep all your dependencies up-to-date. Some older versions of popular JavaScript libraries are known to be vulnerable to Prototype Pollution.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code snippet is incomplete and does not clearly show where the Prototype Pollution vulnerability is. However, if the vulnerability is due to the use of a function that can modify an object's prototype, you should replace that function with a safer alternative.\n\nFor example, if you are using `Object.setPrototypeOf()` to add a property to an object, you can replace it with `Object.assign()`:\n\n```javascript\n// Vulnerable code\nObject.setPrototypeOf(obj, { x: 'polluted' });\n\n// Fixed code\nObject.assign(obj, { x: 'polluted' });\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet does not clearly show any library dependencies.\n\n## References\n\n- [CWE-250: Execution with Unnecessary Privileges](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/250.html)"
