            {
              "id": "0bd56245-a493-3d60-8d7c-8c125c92bccd",
              "name": "XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when an application includes untrusted data in a new web page without proper validation or escaping, or updates an existing web page with user-supplied data using a browser API that can create HTML or JavaScript. XSS allows attackers to execute scripts in the victim's browser which can hijack user sessions, deface web sites, or redirect the user to malicious sites.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the vulnerability lies in the `globalEval` function where it directly evaluates the string `j` without any sanitization or encoding. This can lead to execution of malicious scripts if `j` contains any.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate XSS vulnerabilities, it is recommended to:\n\n- Use frameworks that automatically escape XSS by design, such as Ruby on Rails, React JS, etc.\n- Escape untrusted HTTP request data based on the context in the HTML output (body, attribute, JavaScript, CSS, or URL) will resolve Reflected and Stored XSS vulnerabilities.\n- Apply context-sensitive encoding when modifying the browser document on the client side acts against DOM XSS.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe `globalEval` function should be modified to avoid direct evaluation of the string. If evaluation is necessary, the string should be sanitized before evaluation. Here is a simple fix:\n\n```javascript\nglobalEval: function(j) {\n    if (j && typeof j === 'string') {\n        // Sanitize the string before evaluation\n        j = j.replace(/<script[^>]*?>.*?<\\/script>/gi, '');\n        eval(j);\n    }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code seems to be a part of jQuery library. Therefore, jQuery is required for this code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "041994bc-77d8-3d6f-b0c7-34be57933f27",
              "name": "\"Arbitrary Code Execution via Eval(variable) Detection\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe vulnerability \"Arbitrary Code Execution via Eval(variable) Detection\" in JavaScript refers to the insecure use of the `eval()` function or similar functions like `Function()`, `setTimeout()`, and `setInterval()`. These functions can execute JavaScript code represented as a string, which can lead to arbitrary code execution if the input is not properly sanitized. This can allow an attacker to execute malicious scripts, leading to a variety of attacks such as Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), data theft, or even remote code execution.\n\nIn the provided code snippet, the function `globalEval()` is a potential vulnerability sink as it uses `(new Function(\"return \"+j))()` to execute code from a string. If the variable `j` is controlled by an attacker, it can lead to arbitrary code execution.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, avoid using `eval()` or similar functions whenever possible. If you must use them, ensure that the input is properly sanitized and validated to prevent the execution of malicious code. \n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nInstead of using `(new Function(\"return \"+j))()`, consider using `JSON.parse()` to safely parse JSON data:\n\n```javascript\nparseJSON:function(j){\n  if(typeof j!==\"string\"||!j)return null;\n  j=b.trim(j);\n  if(C.test(j.replace(J,\"@\").replace(w,\"]\").replace(I,\"\"))) {\n    return E.JSON&&E.JSON.parse?E.JSON.parse(j):b.error(\"Invalid JSON: \"+j);\n  } else {\n    b.error(\"Invalid JSON: \"+j);\n  }\n}\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be a part of jQuery library. Therefore, jQuery is required for the code to execute properly.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP - Code Injection](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Code_Injection)\n- [CWE-94: Improper Control of Generation of Code ('Code Injection')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-94",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/94.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-94"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "a9d0fb15-b99d-3c26-bb07-fa03fc5385f7",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a program from an untrusted source and is used to generate dynamic HTML content. The vulnerability here is a potential XSS vulnerability from user-controlled data in an HTML string in JavaScript. This can allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts and have them executed by the victim's browser.\n\nThe provided JavaScript code snippet seems to be handling events and selectors, possibly from a library like jQuery. If any part of this code uses user-controlled data to generate HTML or manipulate the DOM without proper sanitization, it could lead to an XSS vulnerability.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this type of vulnerability, you should:\n\n1. Always validate and sanitize user input: Ensure that user input matches expected patterns and remove or escape any potentially harmful characters.\n\n2. Use output encoding libraries: Libraries like OWASP's Java Encoder for Java or OWASP's JavaScript Sanitizer for JavaScript can automatically escape any potentially harmful characters in user-controlled data.\n\n3. Use Content Security Policy (CSP): CSP can help prevent XSS by restricting where scripts can be loaded from and blocking inline scripts.\n\n4. Avoid using dangerous JavaScript methods: Methods like `eval()`, `innerHTML`, `document.write()`, etc. can lead to XSS if used with user-controlled data.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nWithout the full context of the code, it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, if user-controlled data is being used to generate HTML or manipulate the DOM, it should be properly sanitized. For example, if jQuery is being used, `.text()` can be used instead of `.html()` to safely set the text content of an element.\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe code snippet seems to be using a library like jQuery for handling events and selectors. However, without the full context of the code, it's hard to say what other library dependencies might be required.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-79",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-79"
                ]
              }
