            {
              "id": "9fdc2325-b3a1-3d56-92d3-ce01826ebf5b",
              "name": "Secure Regex Against User Input to Prevent ReDoS Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe \"Secure Regex Against User Input to Prevent ReDoS Vulnerability\" is a security vulnerability in JavaScript that can occur when a regular expression (regex) is used to process user inputs. ReDoS, or Regular Expression Denial of Service, is a type of Denial of Service attack where an attacker provides a malicious input to a regular expression that causes it to take a very long time to evaluate, effectively blocking the event loop and causing the application to hang or become unresponsive.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, it is recommended to avoid using regular expressions that can result in catastrophic backtracking, or to use non-blocking regular expression engines. It is also advisable to limit the length of user input that is processed by a regular expression and to use a timeout when evaluating a regular expression.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code snippet does not seem to contain a regular expression, so it's hard to provide a specific fix. However, here is a general advice on how to secure a regular expression against user input:\n\n```javascript\nlet userInput = /* some user input */;\nlet safeInput = userInput.slice(0, 100); // limit the length of the input\nlet regex = new RegExp(safeInput);\n// use the regex in a safe way, with a timeout\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be using jQuery, so jQuery would be a required library dependency for this code to execute properly.\n\n## OWASP and CWE Resources\n\n- [CWE-400: Uncontrolled Resource Consumption](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html)\n\nPlease note that the links provided are subject to change and may not be accessible at a later date."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-400",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html"
                  }
                ],
                "severity": "MEDIUM",
                "kind": "FAIL",
                "tags": [
                  "CWE-400"
                ]
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "93659125-96bc-3536-9b1d-9c3452260825",
              "name": "Secure Regex Against User Input to Prevent ReDoS Vulnerability",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nThe Regular Expression Denial of Service (ReDoS) is a type of Denial of Service attack. Many Regular Expressions implementations may reach extreme situations that cause them to work very slowly (exponentially related to input size). An attacker can then cause a program using a Regular Expression to enter these extreme situations and then hang for a very long time.\n\nIn JavaScript, the ReDoS vulnerability can occur when user input is passed into a regular expression. If the regular expression is poorly written, it can cause the application to hang, leading to a denial of service.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate against ReDoS attacks, you should:\n\n- Avoid using regular expressions if possible.\n- If you must use regular expressions, make sure they are not susceptible to catastrophic backtracking.\n- Never use user input inside a regular expression without proper sanitization and validation.\n- Use tools to automatically detect potentially vulnerable regular expressions.\n- Consider using a timeout for regular expression processing.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe provided code snippet does not contain a clear regular expression vulnerability, so a specific fix cannot be provided. However, here is a general example of how to fix a ReDoS vulnerability:\n\n```javascript\n// Vulnerable code\nlet re = new RegExp(userInput);\n\n// Fixed code\nlet sanitizedInput = sanitize(userInput); // Implement this function\nlet re = new RegExp(sanitizedInput);\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be using the jQuery library, but it's not clear from the provided snippet alone. \n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP ReDoS](https://owasp.org/www-community/attacks/Regular_expression_Denial_of_Service_-_ReDoS)\n- [CWE-400: Uncontrolled Resource Consumption](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html)\n\nPlease note that the links were active and accessible at the time of writing this report."
              },
              "properties": {
                "cwe": [
                  {
                    "id": "CWE-400",
                    "url": "https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/400.html"
                  }
                ],
