            {
              "id": "daab1497-e94d-3df5-ab29-79bbf8c4322f",
              "name": "\"Potential XSS Vulnerability from User-Controlled Data in HTML String\"",
              "helpUri": "https://www.glog.ai/",
              "help": {
                "text": "",
                "markdown": "## Description\n\nCross-Site Scripting (XSS) vulnerabilities occur when data enters a program from an untrusted source and the data is used to generate dynamic HTML content. The vulnerability here is that the user-controlled data in the HTML string could potentially contain malicious scripts, which would then be executed by the victim's browser. This could lead to a variety of attacks, such as stealing sensitive information, performing actions on behalf of the user, or spreading malware.\n\nIn the provided JavaScript code, the potential XSS vulnerability arises from the use of user-controlled data in the `a.origType` and `a.selector` variables. These variables are used without proper sanitization or encoding, which could allow an attacker to inject malicious scripts.\n\n## Mitigation Advice\n\nTo mitigate this vulnerability, you should always treat user-controlled data as untrusted. Here are some general steps you can take:\n\n1. **Input Validation**: Validate user input on server-side and client-side. Use a whitelist of allowed inputs.\n\n2. **Output Encoding**: When user-controlled data is included in HTML, ensure it is properly HTML-encoded. This will prevent any potentially malicious scripts from being executed.\n\n3. **Use HTTPOnly Cookies**: This will prevent access to cookies via JavaScript.\n\n4. **Content Security Policy (CSP)**: Implementing CSP can effectively reduce XSS risks by declaring which dynamic resources are allowed to load.\n\n## Source Code Fix Recommendation\n\nThe specific fix for the provided code would be to sanitize the `a.origType` and `a.selector` variables before they are used. This can be done using a function like `encodeForHTML()` from the OWASP Java Encoder library for Java or `htmlspecialchars()` function in PHP.\n\n```javascript\nY(encodeForHTML(a.origType), encodeForHTML(a.selector)),a)}},beforeunload:{setup:function(a,b,d){if(c.isWindow(this))this.onbeforeunload=d},teardown:function(a,b){if(this.onbeforeunload===b)this.onbeforeunload=null}}}};c.removeEvent=t.removeEventListener?function(a,b,d){a.removeEventListener&&a.removeEventListener(b,d,false)}:function(a,b,d){a.detachEvent&&a.detachEvent(\"on\"+b,d)};c.Event=function(a){if(!this.preventDefault)return new c.Event(a);if(a&&a.type){this.originalEvent=a;this.type=a.type}else this.type=a;this.timeStamp=\n```\n\n## Library Dependencies\n\nThe provided code snippet seems to be a part of a larger codebase, possibly jQuery or a similar library. The exact dependencies cannot be determined from the provided snippet.\n\n## References\n\n- [OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [OWASP DOM based XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet](https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/DOM_based_XSS_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html)\n- [CWE-79: Improper Neutralization of Input During Web Page Generation ('Cross-site Scripting')](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/79.html)"
